# Citation metadata for SteinerMineBench.
#
# `doi:` and `identifiers:` below carry the Zenodo CONCEPT DOI, which always
# resolves to the newest version -- not the version-specific DOI of any single
# release. Bump it only if Zenodo ever mints a new concept record.
#
# No preferred-citation block: GitHub's "Cite this repository" button and
# most reference managers (including Zenodo) display preferred-citation
# instead of the entry above when one is present, which would surface the
# in-preparation paper -- no journal, no DOI -- as the default citation
# instead of this dataset's working DOI. Once the paper is published, add a
# preferred-citation block back with its `journal` and `doi` filled in.
#
# `python validate.py --publication` re-checks that no unfilled template markers
# have crept back into this file, .zenodo.json or the README.

cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use SteinerMineBench, please cite the dataset. Once the accompanying paper is published, please cite both."
type: dataset

title: "SteinerMineBench: a benchmark suite for geotechnically-weighted Steiner trees on voxel grids"
abstract: >-
  Twenty-eight synthetic, procedurally generated benchmark instances for
  underground mine ramp network optimisation, modelled as directed Steiner
  arborescences on three-dimensional voxel cost grids derived from the NGI
  Barton Q-system. Instances vary along five controlled axes: fault
  architecture crossed with rock mass quality regime, portal location,
  production zone count, voxel resolution from 1.3e5 to 1.3e8 voxels, and
  haulage tonnage. Each bundle ships a compressed cost grid, complete metadata,
  a best-known reference solution, and a provably valid lower bound on each of
  three cost tracks: the raw voxel relaxation, the grade-constrained buildable
  capital cost, and capital plus life-of-mine operating cost. Other researchers
  can therefore run their own solvers on identical instances and report a
  certified optimality gap. Reference solutions are computed on a deterministic
  CPU path and are bit-reproducible on any machine; each bundle records the
  solver engine and environment that produced it. All data is synthetic and is
  not derived from any operating mine.

version: "1.0.0"
date-released: "2026-08-15"
license: CC-BY-4.0
license-url: "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/"

doi: "10.5281/zenodo.22239846"
identifiers:
  - type: doi
    value: "10.5281/zenodo.22239846"
    description: "Concept DOI: always resolves to the most recent version"

repository-code: "https://github.com/1NightCrawler2/steinermine-bench"
url: "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.22239846"

authors:
